# Seven OS

**Seven OS** is a Linux-based operating system project focused on delivering a complete desktop operating system with its own identity, desktop experience, system services, applications, installer, update system, and recovery environment.

> Seven OS is not just a desktop interface. The project is intended to become a complete, installable operating system built on top of the Linux kernel.

## Current Base

The repository currently contains the Linux kernel source tree used as the low-level foundation of Seven OS.

Current kernel base:

- Linux 7.3.0-rc5
- x86_64 as the initial primary desktop target
- C and Rust kernel support
- Linux hardware driver ecosystem
- UEFI-capable platform foundation

The kernel provides the hardware abstraction, process scheduling, virtual memory, filesystems, networking, security infrastructure, and device-driver support required by the rest of Seven OS.

## Architecture

```text
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  Seven OS                   │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                Seven Desktop                │
│   Shell • Launcher • Panel • Notifications  │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                 Seven Apps                  │
│ Files • Settings • Terminal • Store • Tools │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│               Seven Services                │
│ Network • Audio • Session • Update • Pkg    │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│               Seven Userspace               │
│ Init • IPC • Core Utilities • Runtime       │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│               Seven Kernel                  │
│ Linux Kernel + Seven configuration/patches  │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                  Hardware                   │
│ CPU • GPU • NVMe • USB • Wi-Fi • Audio     │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

## Project Goals

Seven OS is being designed to provide:

- A real bootable and installable operating system
- A distinct Seven visual language rather than a copy of another desktop OS
- A modern Wayland-based graphical environment
- A dedicated Seven desktop shell
- Native system settings and administration tools
- A Seven package-management layer
- Reliable system updates with rollback support
- Recovery and safe-boot capabilities
- UEFI installation support
- Strong hardware compatibility through the Linux driver ecosystem
- Security-focused defaults
- A development SDK for Seven applications
- Professional desktop applications included with the system

## Planned Components

The operating system will be developed as a set of clearly separated components.

### Kernel

`seven-kernel`

Responsible for:

- Seven-specific kernel configuration
- Kernel patches when necessary
- Hardware enablement
- Security configuration
- Boot parameters
- Kernel branding
- Stable kernel tracking

### Base System

`seven-base`

Responsible for the core userspace and root filesystem.

Planned areas include:

- Core utilities
- Filesystem layout
- Runtime libraries
- System defaults
- System configuration
- User and permission foundations

### Init and Services

`seven-init` and the Seven service layer.

Responsible for:

- Boot initialization
- Service lifecycle
- Dependency ordering
- Logging
- Failure handling
- Recovery startup

### Seven Desktop

The graphical desktop environment of Seven OS.

Planned components:

- Wayland compositor
- Desktop shell
- Application launcher
- Panel
- Workspaces
- Window management
- Notification center
- Quick settings
- Lock screen
- Login/session experience

The Seven Desktop must have its own design language and interaction model.

### Seven Applications

Core applications planned for the base installation include:

- Files
- Settings
- Terminal
- Software/Package Manager
- System Monitor
- Image Viewer
- Text Editor
- Calculator
- Archive Manager
- Disk Utility
- Logs/Diagnostics

### Package Management

`seven-pkg`

Planned responsibilities:

- Package installation
- Package removal
- Dependency handling
- Repository metadata
- Package verification
- System package upgrades
- Application package management

### Installer

`seven-installer`

Planned support:

- Live environment
- UEFI boot
- Disk selection
- Partitioning
- Filesystem creation
- User creation
- Bootloader configuration
- Installation validation

### Updates

`seven-update`

The update system is expected to support:

- Signed updates
- Kernel updates
- System updates
- Application updates
- Failure recovery
- Rollback where technically possible

### Recovery

`seven-recovery`

Planned capabilities:

- Recovery boot
- Filesystem diagnostics
- Boot repair
- Package repair
- System rollback
- Reset/reinstall options

## Development Strategy

Seven OS will be developed in stages.

### Phase 1 — Bootable Base

Implementation has started.

- [x] Seven-branded kernel tree
- [x] Reproducible x86_64 kernel build entrypoint
- [x] Minimal userspace definition
- [x] Seven root filesystem identity
- [x] Initramfs generation
- [x] QEMU launch tooling
- [ ] Full clean build validation
- [ ] Confirm interactive boot on QEMU
- [ ] Promote the bootstrap userspace to the permanent Seven service layer

### Phase 2 — Graphical System

- Bring up graphics
- Introduce Wayland
- Implement the Seven compositor/shell
- Add input, display, audio, and networking integration

### Phase 3 — Desktop Platform

- Seven settings
- Seven file manager
- Seven terminal
- Session management
- Notifications
- Package-management UI
- Core system applications

### Phase 4 — Installation and Recovery

- Live image
- Graphical installer
- UEFI installation
- Recovery environment
- Update and rollback infrastructure

### Phase 5 — Hardware Validation

Validation on real hardware covering:

- Intel and AMD CPUs
- Intel, AMD, and NVIDIA graphics where supported
- SATA and NVMe storage
- USB
- Ethernet
- Wi-Fi
- Bluetooth
- Audio
- Multi-monitor configurations
- Laptops and desktop PCs

## Kernel Policy

Development kernels may be used while Seven OS is under active development.

Production releases of Seven OS should use a stable kernel line rather than an RC kernel unless there is a documented technical reason to do otherwise.

Seven-specific changes should preferably be maintained as configuration and focused patches instead of unnecessary divergence from upstream Linux. This makes security updates, driver improvements, and future kernel upgrades easier to maintain.

## Languages

Seven OS may use different languages depending on the subsystem.

Primary choices:

- **C** — Linux kernel and low-level integrations
- **Rust** — memory-safe system components and supported kernel work
- **C/C++** — libraries or integrations where technically appropriate
- **Shell/Python** — build and development tooling

Higher-level Seven components may use additional languages when they provide a clear architectural advantage.

## Build Status

The repository is currently at the **bootable base implementation stage**.

The kernel remains at the repository root. The `seven/` tree now defines the first Seven-specific userspace and image-building layer without duplicating or replacing the kernel.

Implemented foundation:

- Seven Kernel branding
- x86_64 kernel build configuration
- Buildroot-based bootstrap userspace
- Seven `/etc/os-release`
- Seven hostname, issue, shell prompt and MOTD
- compressed initramfs generation
- reproducible build entrypoint
- QEMU boot launcher
- Seven WinExec Windows application layer
- Wine 11.0 x86_64/WoW64 compatibility runtime
- NTSYNC and binfmt_misc kernel integration

Build the current developer image with:

```bash
bash seven/build.sh
```

Then boot it with:

```bash
bash seven/run-qemu.sh
```

By default the generated artifacts live outside the source tree in `../Seven-OS-build/images/`.

The full clean build and interactive QEMU boot still need end-to-end validation. After that, Phase 2 begins with the permanent userspace/service layer followed by graphics, Wayland and the Seven Desktop.

Major components still ahead:

- permanent Seven init/service architecture
- graphical stack and Seven Desktop
- core applications
- package management
- installer and UEFI media
- update/rollback infrastructure
- recovery environment
- production ISO/image generation

## Repository Direction

This repository is intended to evolve into the main Seven OS source tree.

As the project grows, Seven-specific code should remain clearly separated from imported upstream source whenever practical. This makes upstream synchronization and maintenance significantly safer.

## License

The Linux kernel source contained in this repository remains subject to its original licensing terms, primarily **GPL-2.0-only**, together with the licensing notices and exceptions included in the upstream source tree.

Seven-specific components may have their own license files where appropriate, but they must remain compatible with the licenses of the code they depend on.

See `COPYING` and the relevant source-file SPDX headers for details.

## Windows Application Compatibility

Seven OS includes a dedicated userspace compatibility entry point for Windows
applications.

```text
.exe / .msi
     ↓
Seven WinExec
     ↓
Seven Windows Runtime
     ↓
Wine / WoW64
     ↓
Win32 / NT APIs
     ↓
Seven userspace
     ↓
Seven Kernel
```

The kernel build now enables `NTSYNC` and `binfmt_misc`. The base userspace
contains `seven-winexec`, PE/MSI registration and isolated per-application
prefix management.

The runtime interface is deliberately independent from Wine itself. This lets
Seven update the compatibility runtime without changing how applications are
launched. The bootstrap build now includes Wine 11.0 as the initial x86_64
runtime and enables its new WoW64 architecture. Graphical Wayland/Vulkan,
audio and gaming-specific integration will be added with the Seven Desktop
stack.

---

**Seven OS** — a complete desktop operating system built with its own identity on a proven Linux foundation.
